    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nWith one or more extra cursors enabled, AND follow-mode disabled, when the main cursor exit insert mode all the other extra cursors move one column to the left.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- In normal mode\n- Place an extra cursor on line 1 at column 3 (using `Q`)\n- Move the main cursor at the second line at any column\n- Enter Insert mode with the main cursor and then exit mode\n\nThe extra cursor is now shifted 1 column to the left\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nWith follow-mode disabled, I would expect the extra cursors to remain unchanged when the main cursor enters or exits insert mode.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev-1644+gaed60b8a53\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nnot available\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nWSL\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nWin Terminal\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nunrelated\n\n### Installation\n\nfrom repo",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> With follow-mode disabled, I would expect the extra cursors to remain unchanged when the main cursor enters or exits insert mode\n\nThen the cursors will be placed differently than the primary, after `i` ...  `i` is not really a \"motion\". Thus it's treated similar to visual-mode \"motions\" ([even with follow-mode off](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/41631)), and edit operations.\n\nOTOH, the current behavior means the user is \"punished\" for an accidental `i`, requiring them to enter follow-mode to fix the \"mistake\".\n\n\nAre you expecting a no-edit `i` to be special-cased? What do you expect if an edit is done in insert-mode?",
