        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> If follow mode worked as you imagined, where it controlled whether _anything_ was replayed with the other cursors, I don't think it would be very useful, and people would just leave it on. The current mechanism is balanced around letting you place cursors one at a time, _and_ not requiring follow mode for every little thing.\n\nSo, there must be some kind of communication issue here. I'm not questioning the usefulness of being able to enable or disable follow-mode; I'm just saying that, from a user's perspective, its behavior is confusing.\n\nAs I already pointed out:\n\n> With follow-mode disabled, some operations are replicated (e.g. dw), while movements are not.\n> \n> I also find it counterintuitive that, with follow-mode disabled, entering Visual mode makes the other cursors start following the main cursor. If follow-mode is disabled... why are they moving?\n\nI understand the technical distinction between \"operations\" and \"motions\", but the fact remains that, with follow-mode disabled, some things are replicated while others aren't. And from a user's perspective, that's objectively strange (even if it may not seem that way to Neovim contributors).",
